<TopicPage slug="subjects/religious-studies/intermediate/hinduism">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Hinduism is the <strong>oldest continuously practised religious family</strong> — 4,000 years
    of Vedic ritual, philosophy and devotion, with no founder, no single church and no single
    creed. Its unity is structural: shared concepts (dharma, karma, moksha), shared authority
    (the Vedas), and an astonishing internal diversity of gods, practices and philosophies.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the family structure, the core concepts, and the textual layers" widget="equationStep" config={hinduCfg} />

  <h2>What scholars emphasise</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>"Hinduism" as umbrella:</strong> the single-word label is colonial-era convenience; insiders historically identified by sampradaya (tradition) — Shaiva, Vaishnava, Shakta. The 2011 Indian census recorded dozens of distinct traditions under the label.</li>
    <li><strong>Philosophy is central, not folkloric:</strong> the six orthodox schools (Nyaya, Vaisheshika, Samkhya, Yoga, Mimamsa, Vedanta) argued logic, epistemology and metaphysics with a rigour Western philosophy ignored until recently.</li>
    <li><strong>Bhakti democratised:</strong> the medieval devotional movements (c. 600–1700 CE) opened access beyond caste and ritual expertise — poetry in local languages, direct devotion, poet-saints from every stratum.</li>
    <li><strong>Caste is part of the history, contested within:</strong> varna/jati hierarchies are embedded in some classical texts; bhakti, reform movements (Rammohan Roy, Ambedkar's conversion to Buddhism, 1956) and modern Dalit theology challenge them from within the tradition's own resources.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Flood, An Introduction to Hinduism; Doniger, The Hindus: An Alternative History; Bhagavad Gita (trans. numerous).</p>
</TopicPage>
